## maximum queue times and run times
## these times are in hours *since last status update*
## status updates generally happen somewhat frequently
## if UDP messaging from the cluster isn't working, then status updates will be missed
## set to zero to disable

$global_max_queue_time_hours = 0;
$global_max_run_time_hours   = 0;

## abandonment ceiling, in hours since last status update.
##
## gridctl defers the stall timers above while a cluster is unreachable. This
## caps that deferral: past this many hours the job is closed out as a
## timeout, with autoflow status CLUSTER_UNAVAILABLE so the reason is not
## confused with a job failure. Zero defers indefinitely.
##
## Make it comfortably longer than a routine maintenance window: it should
## catch a site that is gone, not a site that is down for the afternoon.
## cluster_probe_outage_verdict() in gridctl explains the policy.
$global_cluster_abandon_hours = 72;

## after a job reports COMPLETE, jobmonitor waits for the cluster's (UDP)
## "Finished" message before finalizing.  Because UDP is unreliable, it will
## finalize anyway once $global_complete_grace_seconds have elapsed since the
## job was first seen COMPLETE (measured on the jobmonitor's own clock, so it
## is immune to timezone/clock skew between the LIMS host, cluster, and DB).
## $global_complete_max_seconds is a hard ceiling: a job stuck COMPLETE past
## this is force-finalized.  If unset, jobmonitor defaults to 600 and 21600.

$global_complete_grace_seconds = 600;
$global_complete_max_seconds   = 21600;

## sbatch is deliberately submitted once. An SSH failure can happen after
## Slurm accepted the job but before its ID reached LIMS, so retry/backoff here
## would risk creating a duplicate. Reconcile an uncertain submission on the
## scheduler before asking LIMS to submit it again.

## Remote execution budgets, retries and breaker policy are defined in
## class/remote_exec.php. GNU timeout is required for wall-clock limits.

## Maximum age of cluster health records in seconds. Stale records are
## treated as down. Set to a few probe intervals, or zero to disable.
$global_cluster_status_max_age_seconds = 900;


